{
  "_meta": {
    "title": "Chinese era names along a single dynastic line",
    "scope": "330 era names covering 140 BCE to 1911 CE, from Jianyuan to the third year of Xuantong. One era name is given for each year, taken from a single line of succession; contending regimes are omitted.",
    "line": "Western Han, Xin, Gengshi, Eastern Han, Wei, Jin, the southern Song, Qi, Liang and Chen, Sui, Tang (with the Wu Zhou interval as Zhou), the Five Dynasties, Song, Yuan, Ming, Qing. The Sixteen Kingdoms, Liao, Jin, Western Xia, the Ten Kingdoms and the Southern Ming are not collected.",
    "yearSpace": "Astronomical year numbering, which includes a year 0, so a year N BCE is written as 1 - N.",
    "fields": "y = the calendar year this entry takes effect. s = the year the era itself began, present only when it differs from y, which is what makes 589 the ninth year of Kaihuang rather than its first. d/dt = dynasty in Simplified and Traditional Chinese. n/nt = era name in Simplified and Traditional Chinese.",
    "provenance": "Compiled and checked by hand against the standard Chinese calendar concordances. Historical chronology is not itself copyrightable; the choice of a single line of succession, the field design and the transcription are original to this project.",
    "count": {"eras": 330},
    "license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0",
    "updated": "2026-07-17",
    "caveats": [
      "An era rarely changed at the turn of the year, so one calendar year often spans two era names. The table gives the one in principal use that year, which leaves a known error of one year at the boundary: 220 is entered as Huangchu rather than Yankang, 684 as Guangzhai, 700 as Jiushi.",
      "The table does not distinguish an era counted from the year of accession from one counted from the year after. Year y is always the first year of its era.",
      "The Wu Zhou interval, 690 to 704, is carried under the dynasty Zhou.",
      "Entries are in ascending order of y, and each applies until the year given by the next entry."
    ]
  },
